# Erdős Problem #99 — exact chamber-interface baseline and residual gluing

**Date:** 2026-09-14
**Repository base:** `1bcd5008`
**State version:** `2026-09-14.audit.51`
**Run type:** closing-proof reduction / exact chamber decomposition
**Target:** Target C-II, canonical full-core Type-II branch
**Claim class:** proof-only consequence awaiting independent audit
**External literature:** no new import
**Computation:** exact-rational regression only

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

The current `q-k` chamber bootstrap proves

\[
\Sigma_{ch}/D\ge2.1586344\ldots-o(1)
\]

by separately weakening the chamber area, perimeter, and chamber-count terms. Those
three terms have an exact cancellation that gives a substantially stronger result.

Let

\[
d:=b-P_F\ge0,
\qquad
H:=d+Q+2f,
\qquad
J:=d+Q+4f.
\tag{CB.1}
\]

The latest bootstrap gives

\[
\liminf J/D\ge j_{171}=2.489387211845480\ldots .
\tag{CB.2}
\]

Define the exact chamber absorption residual

\[
\boxed{
\mathcal R_{ch}
:=(A_{ch}-a_0Z_{ch})-c_0H\ge0,
}
\tag{CB.3}
\]

where `a_0=sqrt(3)/2` and `c_0=a_0/2`.

## 0. Relation to the existing force-hull residual theorem

The quantity introduced below is not a new internal residual. The September 12
proof-only theorem `target_c_inactive_chamber_residual_conservation.md` already defines

\[
R_F:=\Sigma_F-{E_4\over a_0}
={A_F\over a_0}+{P_F\over2}+1-N_F-{E_4\over a_0}\ge0
\]

and proves that it is the sum of the chamber fixed-boundary residuals. With the
notation of this note,

\[
\boxed{{\mathcal R_{ch}\over a_0}=R_F.}
\]

Accordingly, the new content here is the exact propagation of the latest `J` floor
into the raw chamber slack and the exterior-cap/full-source specialization in Section
3. The internal residual conservation itself retains its earlier provenance.

## 1. Exact chamber perimeter above the interface currency

Canonical Section 77 gives

\[
P_{ch}=(4f+Q)+(b+P_F-2q+2a).
\tag{CB.4}
\]

Subtract `J` and simplify:

\[
\begin{aligned}
P_{ch}-J
&=2P_F-2q+2a\\
&=2(P_F-q+a).
\end{aligned}
\tag{CB.5}
\]

Canonical Section 67 writes

\[
P_F=q-a+L_{add},
\tag{CB.6}
\]

where `L_add` is the total Euclidean length of the `a` force-hull sides added to close
the old outer contact face. Hence

\[
\boxed{
P_{ch}=J+2L_{add}.
}
\tag{CB.7}
\]

Every added hull side joins distinct source points, so `L_add>=a` and in particular
`L_add>=0`.

## 2. Exact chamber slack decomposition

The Section-77 chamber slack is

\[
\Sigma_{ch}
={A_{ch}-a_0Z_{ch}\over a_0}+{P_{ch}\over2}+s_{ch},
\qquad s_{ch}=f+a.
\tag{CB.8}
\]

By (CB.3) and `c_0/a_0=1/2`,

\[
{A_{ch}-a_0Z_{ch}\over a_0}
={H\over2}+{\mathcal R_{ch}\over a_0}.
\tag{CB.9}
\]

Insert (CB.7)--(CB.9):

\[
\Sigma_{ch}
={H\over2}+{J\over2}+f
+{\mathcal R_{ch}\over a_0}+L_{add}+a.
\]

But `J-H=2f`, so

\[
{H\over2}+{J\over2}+f=J.
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\Sigma_{ch}
=J+{\mathcal R_{ch}\over a_0}+L_{add}+a.
}
\tag{CB.10}
\]

All three residual terms after `J` are nonnegative. Thus the finite inequality

\[
\boxed{\Sigma_{ch}\ge J}
\tag{CB.11}
\]

holds throughout the canonical post-ZF scope where these ledgers are defined.

In particular, the latest §171 bootstrap immediately improves to

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {\Sigma_{ch}\over D}
\ge j_{171}
=2.489387211845480\ldots .
}
\tag{CB.12}
\]

This supersedes the proof-only `2.1586344129...` chamber-slack floor and also the
`2.2392949006...` floor obtained from the first Oler-gluing note.

## 3. The residual gluing identity is much cleaner than the raw one

The exact Oler-gluing theorem at `3ed76f59` proves

\[
\Sigma_{ch}+\Sigma_{ext}
=\Omega_K+2(T-q/2)+2a+L_{cap}.
\tag{CB.13}
\]

The exact all-train endpoint identity is

\[
J=2T-P_F.
\tag{CB.14}
\]

Therefore

\[
2(T-q/2)=J+P_F-q.
\tag{CB.15}
\]

From (CB.6), `P_F-q=L_add-a`. Substitute (CB.10), (CB.15) into (CB.13):

\[
J+{\mathcal R_{ch}\over a_0}+L_{add}+a+\Sigma_{ext}
=
\Omega_K+J+L_{add}-a+2a+L_{cap}.
\]

Every forced train/interface term cancels exactly, leaving

\[
\boxed{
{\mathcal R_{ch}\over a_0}+\Sigma_{ext}
=\Omega_K+L_{cap}.
}
\tag{CB.16}
\]

This is the correct additive gluing invariant.

The mandatory linear stopped-train surplus is not an unexplained error term. It is
exactly the baseline `J` already present in the chamber area/perimeter/count ledger.
After subtracting that baseline, the remaining chamber packing residual plus exterior
cap Oler slack equals the punctured full-source packing slack plus the excess length of
exterior-cap closing chords.

## 4. Structural consequences

Equation (CB.16) gives two finite upper bounds that were not explicit before:

\[
\boxed{
{\mathcal R_{ch}\over a_0}
\le\Omega_K+L_{cap},
\qquad
\Sigma_{ext}\le\Omega_K+L_{cap}.
}
\tag{CB.17}
\]

It also identifies exact equality cases.

- If `F=K`, then there are no exterior caps, `Sigma_ext=L_cap=0`, and
  \[
  \mathcal R_{ch}/a_0=\Omega_K.
  \]
- If every exterior closing chord is a unit segment, then `L_cap=0`, so the chamber
  residual and exterior-cap slack partition the single global punctured slack exactly.
- If additionally `Omega_K=o(D)`, then both residuals are `o(D)` and
  \[
  \Sigma_{ch}=J+L_{add}+a+o(D).
  \]

No one of these stronger hypotheses is asserted for all Type-II survivors.

## 5. Revised C-II target

The current full-core frontier now has an exact two-layer decomposition:

```text
mandatory interface layer:
    Sigma_ch >= J,
    J/D >= 2.4893872118... - o(1);

packing residual layer:
    R_ch/a0 + Sigma_ext = Omega_K + L_cap.
```

Thus an additive gluing argument should not try to upper-bound the whole
`Sigma_ch`. The meaningful quantity is `R_ch`, after the forced interface baseline
`J` has been removed.

A closing theorem can now target one of the following sharply defined statements:

1. an upper bound on `Omega_K+L_cap` incompatible with a separately forced lower bound
   on `R_ch/a0+Sigma_ext`;
2. a geometric theorem showing the linear family of exceptional chambers forces
   `R_ch=Omega(D)` with coefficient larger than the global residual budget;
3. a source-preserving diameter descent when `L_add+a` or `L_cap` is linear; or
4. rigidity of the near-equality regime where all four residuals in (CB.16) are small.

The macroscopic-container branch remains separate.

## 6. Scope audit

- `P_ch` is the sum of the perimeters of the Section-77 internal inactive chambers;
  no exterior-cap perimeter is included in it.
- `L_add` is Section 67's actual Euclidean length of added force-hull closing sides.
- `T` is the complete stopped-train count, including singleton trains.
- `R_ch>=0` uses exactly the two Section-67 Oler absorption inequalities combined in
  canonical Section 85; it is not a new geometric assumption.
- `Omega_K` and `L_cap` are the quantities already hostile-audited at `1bcd5008`.
- All source points are retained.

**Disposition:** proof-only exact decomposition and quantitative strengthening. C-II
remains open.
